{
  "title": "Three things self-hosting exposed",
  "purpose": "A write-up for anyone standing up their own deployment, so the next person who hits these three symptoms does not spend the afternoon debugging the thing the symptom pointed at. No answers needed; this is a reference page to keep and hand on.",
  "resumeHint": "Re-download the MCP client from your own deployment (not the reference host), re-copy the `claude mcp add` line from your /start page, then restart with your real env file and confirm the loader refuses anything it cannot parse.",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "Three things self-hosting exposed",
        "lead": "Self-hosting surfaced three defects that had been invisible on the reference deployment. Each one presented as a symptom that pointed somewhere other than its cause: a signing bug that was an addressing bug, a broken shell command that was a template-literal bug, and a timeout that was silently not the timeout you set. All three are fixed. This page is so you recognise them if you meet them in an older build, a fork, or a copy of the instructions taken by hand.",
        "points": [
          "`unauthorized` on every call meant the client was signing correctly and talking to the wrong host.",
          "A `claude mcp add` command that arrived as one joined line was a line continuation eaten by a template literal, not a formatting slip.",
          "MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s quietly became 90 seconds instead of 90000 milliseconds, in a loader whose whole posture is refusing loudly.",
          "A fourth, unrelated-looking class of bug — a page stylesheet redefining a shared class — now has a test, because twice it was caught by eye and eyes are not a method."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "comparison",
      "props": {
        "title": "Symptom, first guess, actual cause",
        "columns": [
          "What you see",
          "Where it points",
          "What it actually is"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "`unauthorized` on every MCP call, immediately, with a key you just minted",
            "Broken request signing; clock skew; key not saved",
            "The client's default base URL is hardcoded to the reference deployment, so your key is being presented to a host that has never seen it"
          ],
          [
            "The `claude mcp add` command from /start arrives as one long joined line",
            "Copy/paste mangling; the terminal; the browser",
            "A lone trailing backslash inside a template literal is a JS line continuation — the backslash is consumed at build time and never reaches the page"
          ],
          [
            "Long polls time out far sooner than configured; MAX_AWAIT_MS looks set",
            "The tunnel, the proxy, a 524 at the edge",
            "The value did not parse, the loader fell back to its default without saying so, and nothing you set was ever in effect"
          ]
        ],
        "caption": "The shared shape: in each case the symptom is produced by a component downstream of the actual defect, and that component is innocent."
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "1. The client shipped somebody else's address"
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "The MCP client file hardcoded this deployment as its default base URL. A self-hoster who followed /start by hand installed a client that signed every request to somebody else's host — with a key that host had never issued and could not verify. The server did the only correct thing and returned `unauthorized`."
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "warn",
      "text": "`unauthorized` on every single call, from the very first one, reads like a broken signature. It is worth checking the address before the signature: an auth failure that is total and immediate is more often the wrong door than the wrong key."
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "The fix: the instance now substitutes its own URL into the client when it serves the file, the way /cli already worked. A client downloaded from your deployment talks to your deployment. The /cli path had been doing this correctly all along, which is why nobody noticed the other one was not."
    },
    {
      "kind": "list",
      "title": "How to tell whether you have an affected client",
      "items": [
        "Open the installed client file and grep for a base URL you did not choose.",
        "If it names a host that is not yours, re-download it from your own deployment rather than editing it — the served copy is now correct.",
        "Check your own server logs too. If the failing calls never appear there at all, the requests are going somewhere else, and that is the whole answer."
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "danger",
      "text": "If you front your deployment with a CDN that caches by file extension, the substituted client is exactly the kind of file that gets cached and served to everyone. Serve it with no-store, or bust the cache after deploying, or you will hand out a correct file exactly once."
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "2. A trailing backslash that was never a backslash"
    },
    {
      "widget": "annotated-code",
      "props": {
        "title": "The line continuation nobody wrote",
        "notes": [
          {
            "note": "The /start page built its install command inside a template literal. The intent was a shell line continuation for readability. What JavaScript saw was its own line continuation: backslash-newline inside a template literal produces neither a backslash nor a newline. Both were consumed at build time, and the page received one joined line.",
            "language": "javascript",
            "code": "const cmd = `claude mcp add sirdocalot \\\n  --transport http ${baseUrl}/mcp`;\n\n// cmd === \"claude mcp add sirdocalot   --transport http https://…/mcp\"\n// The backslash is gone. The newline is gone. Nothing warned."
          },
          {
            "note": "The fix is to escape the backslash so one survives into the output string, or to stop relying on the literal's newlines and join the parts explicitly. The second reads better because the intent is visible.",
            "language": "javascript",
            "code": "const cmd = [\n  \"claude mcp add sirdocalot\",\n  `  --transport http ${baseUrl}/mcp`,\n].join(\" \\\\\\n\");\n\n// or, inline: `… sirdocalot \\\\\\n  --transport http …`"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "text": "The joined command still runs. That is why it survived: it is a cosmetic failure that looks like a paste problem, so everybody blames their terminal and moves on. Anywhere you generate a multi-line shell snippet from a template literal, this is waiting."
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "3. A number that did not parse, and a loader that said nothing"
    },
    {
      "widget": "annotated-code",
      "props": {
        "title": "The silent fallback",
        "notes": [
          {
            "note": "The loader coerced the environment variable and used a default on anything falsy. `MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s` coerces to NaN, NaN is falsy, and the default was applied. The operator had asked for ninety seconds and, by coincidence of units, got ninety seconds — but only because the default happened to be 90000ms. Any other typo would have produced the default just as quietly, and any other default would have produced a mystery.",
            "language": "javascript",
            "code": "const maxAwaitMs = Number(process.env.MAX_AWAIT_MS) || 90_000;\n\n// MAX_AWAIT_MS=\"90s\"  -> NaN -> 90_000\n// MAX_AWAIT_MS=\"\"     -> 0   -> 90_000\n// MAX_AWaIT_MS=\"…\"    -> unset -> 90_000, and the typo is in the key"
          },
          {
            "note": "This sat in a loader whose entire stated posture is refusing loudly on bad configuration. A single `||` opted one variable out of that posture. It refuses now: present-but-unparseable is an error, absent is the default, and the two are no longer the same thing.",
            "language": "javascript",
            "code": "function numberFromEnv(key, fallback) {\n  const raw = process.env[key];\n  if (raw === undefined || raw === \"\") return fallback;\n  const n = Number(raw);\n  if (!Number.isFinite(n)) {\n    throw new Error(\n      `${key}: expected a number of milliseconds, got ${JSON.stringify(raw)}`\n    );\n  }\n  return n;\n}"
        }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "warn",
      "text": "The general rule this cost us: `Number(x) || default` collapses three distinct states — unset, empty, and garbage — into one, and hides the only one you needed to hear about. Treat absent and invalid as different, always."
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "And a fourth: the bug that had been found by eye twice"
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "A page stylesheet redefined a class that the shared stylesheet already defines. The gallery page defined `.label` — reasonably, for a gallery label — and `.label` is what the block renderer calls a field label. So every input inside a preview rendered uppercase and tiny. Nothing errored. Nothing logged. It was noticed by looking at it, which had also been how it was noticed the previous time."
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "Why this one gets a test and the others get a fix",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "Twice is a class, not an incident",
            "detail": "The same shape of failure — a page-scoped stylesheet shadowing a shared class name — has now bitten twice, in two different pages, with two different class names. A third is a matter of time and of how many pages exist."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "Found by eye is not a method",
            "detail": "Both times, the only detector was a human looking at a rendering and thinking it looked wrong. That scales with neither the number of pages nor the number of people who are not looking closely today."
          },
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "The guard",
            "detail": "A test asserts that no page stylesheet defines a class selector that the shared stylesheet also defines. It reads the CSS, extracts class names per file, and fails on any intersection with the shared set — naming the file, the class, and the shared rule it would shadow."
          },
          {
            "tone": "success",
            "title": "The guard is proved to bite before it is trusted",
            "detail": "Before the fix landed, the test was run against the broken tree and confirmed to fail on the gallery's `.label`. A guard that has never been observed failing is a guard that might be asserting nothing at all; you cannot tell the two apart by watching it pass."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "text": "If you fork the styles, keep this test. If you add a page stylesheet, expect it to tell you off — that is the point. And if you write any guard of your own, break the code on purpose once and watch the guard notice, before you let it into the suite."
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "Quick reference for self-hosters",
        "facts": [
          {
            "key": "unauthorized on every call",
            "value": "Check the base URL baked into your client before you check the key. Re-download the client from your own deployment."
          },
          {
            "key": "Cached client file",
            "value": "The client is now URL-substituted per instance. Do not let an edge cache serve one deployment's copy to another."
          },
          {
            "key": "Install command on one line",
            "value": "Cosmetic; the command still works. Fixed at source by escaping the continuation rather than relying on the template literal."
          },
          {
            "key": "Timeouts shorter than configured",
            "value": "Units, not infrastructure. The loader now refuses an unparseable value instead of defaulting past it."
          },
          {
            "key": "Uppercase, tiny form labels",
            "value": "A page stylesheet is shadowing a shared class. There is a test for this now; run it."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
